  "response": "Disputes of this kind are not settled by asking which team ran the more careful study; they are settled by building replications whose structure makes the two rival explanations empirically separable. The facial feedback hypothesis supplies the clearest worked example. The original 1988 experiment enrolled more than 500 participants, and its finding stood for nearly three decades before a coordinated multi-site replication was finally mounted. That seventeen laboratories contributed data to the 2016 Registered Replication Report is what gave multi-laboratory designs their capacity to permit estimation of heterogeneity in effect sizes across sites. Variation of that kind is the central diagnostic: if unmeasured local conditions carry the effect, laboratories should diverge sharply from one another, whereas if the effect is simply not there, they should converge on a null.\n\nThe outcome was unambiguous. The pooled effect estimate across the seventeen replication laboratories was indistinguishable from zero; in other words, the combined replication data showed no detectable influence of pen position on funniness ratings, and it is the material assembled by those seventeen contributing laboratories that establishes this absence. Defenders of the original result answered on procedural rather than statistical grounds. Fritz Strack reviewed and approved the replication protocol before data collection began, and he later argued that the video cameras recording participants undermined the replication attempt. A design signed off on in advance by its own originator cannot be squared with the charge that the recording arrangements it specified spoiled the test.\n\nThe adjudication then turns on what a null of this precision licenses. Because no trace of a pen-position influence survived pooling, the evidence indicates that the original 1988 estimate of the effect was larger than the true population effect. The complaint about recording equipment amounts, restated in more general terms, to the claim that the replication sessions contained procedural features that were absent from the original 1988 sessions. At least one of these two accounts holds, and perhaps both do: either the first estimate ran ahead of reality, or the later sessions introduced something the earlier ones lacked. Noah, Schul and Mayo published a direct test of the camera hypothesis in 2018. Low between-laboratory heterogeneity weakens the argument that local procedural variation explains a null replication.\n\nSeveral further particulars belong to the record. The original and replication studies both used cartoons drawn by Gary Larson. Noah and colleagues reported a facial feedback effect in sessions conducted without cameras. Strack, by contrast, endorsed the conclusion that his 1988 finding was a false positive. The lesson is methodological. What resolves a contest between falsity and infidelity is never a declaration about who was faithful to the original design, but a chain of evidence: a pre-registered pooled estimate, a direct measure of dispersion across sites, and an experiment aimed squarely at the procedural difference alleged to matter.",
